00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280 align:start position:0%
 
I spoke with Secretary of State Mike  Pompeo earlier today about the peace  agreement and the Afghan president's  refusal to release Taliban prisoners I  started by asking if that happens could  it void the New Deal there's a long  series of discussions have been taking  place over months and months and it  shouldn't surprise anyone that the  habits of old days are hard to break and  this will be a bumpy road going forward  if you look at the agreement as a  provision about the exchange of  prisoners talks about discussions with  all the relevant parties and moving  forward we have a team on the ground  even as we're sitting here now working  working to deliver not only on that  element right that's a important  confidence-building measure but  delivering on the larger task which is  to keep the levels of violence down and  getting the teams built so that the  first time in 20 years something the  previous two administrations couldn't  achieve a commitment to break with  al-qaeda and a commitment to sit down  with the other side has not been done  before work that that's the next step  that's the next goal I'm confident it'll  be challenged but we're determined to  get there this morning the Taliban  through a spokesman said they were  resuming operations against the Afghan  government and its forces not US troops  that's what they said in response to the  president about the Taliban prisoner  issue how is that gonna work I've seen  lots of remarks just watch what really  happens pay less attention to statements  pay less attention to things people say  watch what happens on the ground there  there's been a lot of work done at  detailed levels about how this will  proceed so far so good  or just hours into this I'm sure we'll  have days when we start and say that the  problems big and but we're determined  and presidents made this commitment our  mission set is to protect America from  the threat of terrorism from Afghanistan  to reduce our cost I was a CIA director  I was a soldier I want to see fewer  Americans having to make their third  fourth and fifth trip there and fewer  Americans coming home injured maimed or  worship never having a chance to be with  their loved one against that's the  president's mission that's what this  agreement is aiming to achieve the  president as a candidate was very  critical of President Obama setting a  timeline and talked about it a lot  you're setting a timeline for withdrawal  of US troops it's very different what  we've done is fundamentally different  than what the Obama administration did  indeed we accomplished what they tried  to do but could not they never got the  Taliban to break with al-qaeda and they  never got a commitment that says if you  execute the following conditions based  that is if the violence levels come down  that is if the security posture for the  United States of America is reduced then  and only then will begin to deliver a  commensurate footprint inside of your  country you can see the deal we've made  it all public there's a there's no  secrets ideals members of Congress will  get to see the to classified  implementation military elements of this  but the deal is laid out there for the  world to see I'm confident that over  time we're gonna get to a better place  there's a president Ghani said this  weekend the most important verification  that needs to take place is not just  Taliban cutting ties with al Qaeda but  with all terrorist groups in our  discussion that is going to be one of  the most fundamental issues this  requires verification not verbal  assurances as you know the Taliban has a  long history with al Qaeda it's detailed  in the 9/11 Commission reports this  weekend on Face the Nation on CBS you  said that it was a strict cut and you  said that that the Taliban would now  work against Al Qaeda and they've made a  break  al Qaeda's mentioned twice in the  passages in the report in the language  it says it will not allow any of its  members or other individuals or groups  including al-qaeda to use the soil of  Afghanistan to threaten the security of  the United States and its allies but  that's not really a break that's not a  very announcing or announcing about  absolute when you say you're not gonna  let him operate there you know Brett  they've done it for 20 years they're not  gonna let them operate it any more  that's that as a central provision  that's one that for 20 years I know  ambassador Lee lozad was there trying to  get exactly that language for two  decades we got it  but what's more important than the  language Brett is the execution  President Ghani here's right  verification matters we'll have the  ability to see what they're doing are  they really living up to that commitment  it's our expectation I I met with him  myself when I was in Doha I look them in  the eye  they revalidated that commitment now  they've got executed now we'll be able  to see the world we'll be able to see if  they truly live up to that obligation  it's important because that's the reason  we went there Brett you know that I  still think about 9/11 and you can feel  you can feel the emotion of that day I  know all Americans can we went there to  defeat al-qaeda we have crushed them and  we put real real costs on the Taliban  over the past months it's why they came  to the table it's why we're having these  discussion that's why I am hopeful that  all parties in Afghanistan will take  seriously the commitment to try to reach  the political resolution that is the  only path forward for the Afghans so  what do you say to skeptical America and  who looks at this and ahead of an  election and says that this was about  living up to a election promise and not  really about the specifics in a deal on  the ground yeah well they're wrong but  besides that promises do matter we're  trying to live up to all the commitments  that we make it's a good thing that the  president made this commitment it's a  good thing we're on the path but that's  not why this timing took place it took  us longer than we had wished to reach  this point these were hard-fought  negotiations the Taliban expressed  interest in having these negotiations  when President Trump gave the Department  of Defense the authority to really go  after them in serious ways and it took  us this long we now have a timeline this  timeline extends past the election and  it is conditions-based  we've got to get it right Brett we have  an obligation to get this right for the  American people we've got to keep  America safe from attacks in Afghanistan  we got to get the footprint right so we  have the resources capabilities and  skills to do what President Trump is  committed to we took down al Baghdadi we  took our customer Rin we took down Qasem  Soleimani we took down Hamza bin Laden  the president has a clear record on  keeping America safe when we determined  to continue to do that everywhere  including Afghanistan John Bolton former  national security advisors calls this an  Obama style deal I know you push back on  that earlier he also tweeted out North  Korea true to form resumes ballistic  missile testing yet again violating UN  Security Council resolutions US policy  must finally face the reality that North  Korea will never  voluntarily stop pursuing deliverable  nuclear weapons see right presidents  made a real commitment to try to find  the path for Jeremy Kim now goodness  almost two years ago made a commitment  to denuclearize we're determined to try  and achieve that the path forward for  the North Koreans is very clear we are  hopeful they will choose it and we're  using every diplomatic tool we have in  our tool bag last thing coronavirus are  you considering moving staff anyplace  besides China out of a spreading  coronavirus environment Italy Europe  other places it's a good question bred  look it's the vice-president and  secretaries are running the elements of  this we're working with them to make  sure we get it right we want to keep our  team safe not only our diplomats and all  the people that fall under the State  Department in countries around the world  but we want to make sure we keep our  soldiers sailors airmen Marines safe as  well it's a complicated decision making  process we're constantly evaluating both  the right number of people to have and  where we can have them mr. secretary we  appreciate the time great thank you  Brian

